---
import BaseLayout from "../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro";
const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL.replace(/\/$/, "");
---

<BaseLayout title="Transposition in Excel">
  <header class="wrap page-intro">
    <p class="eyebrow">GUIDE 02 / ARRANGEMENT</p>
    <h1>
      Put the letters
      <br />
      in their place<span class="accent">.</span>
    </h1>
    <p>Build a transposition workbench in Excel, then try a new column order in seconds.</p>
    <div class="guide-meta">
      <span>20 MIN READ</span>
      <span>EXCEL 365 / 2024</span>
      <a href={`${base}/decks/transposition/`}>Open this deck ↗</a>
    </div>
  </header>
  <div class="wrap article-layout">
    <nav class="article-toc" aria-label="Guide contents">
      <p class="eyebrow">IN THIS GUIDE</p>
      <a href="#method">01 / Pick a method</a>
      <a href="#grid">02 / Build the grid</a>
      <a href="#manual">03 / Move columns or rows</a>
      <a href="#key">04 / Use a key</a>
      <a href="#decipher">05 / Reverse it</a>
      <a href="#check">06 / Check your work</a>
    </nav>
    <article class="guide-content">
      <h2 id="method">First, agree on the route</h2>
      <p>
        Transposition rearranges letters. The frequency chart stays the same. Here we use five
        columns and the numeric key <code>53124</code>, meaning “read columns 5, 3, 1, 2, 4”.
      </p>
      <pre>Plaintext: WEAREDISCOVERED
Write across five columns:
1 2 3 4 5
W E A R E
D I S C O
V E R E D

Reorder every block (53124): EAWER OSDIC DRVEE
Read down columns (53124):   EOD ASR WDV EIE RCE</pre>
      <p>
        These are different methods. The workbench offers both. The final row stays short; there is
        no automatic X padding. Five-letter output groups are only display spacing and are
        independent of the transposition block size.
      </p>
      <h2 id="grid">Build the grid in Excel</h2>
      <p>
        Create sheets called <strong>Setup</strong>, <strong>Grid</strong> and{" "}
        <strong>Output</strong>. These formulas use English Excel names and commas. Your locale may
        require semicolons instead.
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li>
          On Setup, paste the message into <strong>B1</strong>. Put the block size{" "}
          <strong>5</strong> in <strong>B2</strong>.
        </li>
        <li>In Setup!B3, remove ordinary spaces, line breaks and tabs, and convert to capitals:</li>
      </ol>
      <pre>=UPPER(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(SUBSTITUTE(B1," ",""),CHAR(10),""),CHAR(13),""),CHAR(9),""))</pre>
      <p>
        This cleanup does not remove all punctuation. For this exercise paste A–Z letters and
        whitespace only. Alternatively copy continuous output from the site's cipher tools.
      </p>
      <p>
        On Grid, type the original column numbers <strong>1, 2, 3, 4, 5</strong> into A1:E1. Put
        this formula in A2:
      </p>
      <pre>=MID(Setup!$B$3,(ROW()-2)*Setup!$B$2+COLUMN(),1)</pre>
      <p>
        Fill A2 across to E2 and then down. The required number of data rows is{" "}
        <code>=ROUNDUP(LEN(Setup!$B$3)/Setup!$B$2,0)</code>. For WEAREDISCOVERED that is three data
        rows, A2:E4. Empty final cells return an empty string.
      </p>
      <h2 id="manual">Fix the values before moving them</h2>
      <div class="callout">
        <p>
          <strong>Freeze Panes does not freeze formulas.</strong> Copy Grid!A1:E4 into a new sheet
          called Manual using <strong>Paste Special → Values</strong>. Moving these fixed letters
          will not recalculate their positions. Keep Grid as the untouched source.
        </p>
      </div>
      <p>
        In desktop Excel for Windows, select a column header on Manual, point at the selection
        border, hold <strong>Shift</strong> and drag it between columns. Look for the insertion
        line. Move the columns until their original-number headers read 5, 3, 1, 2, 4. The Shift key
        inserts rather than overwriting cells. A reliable alternative is Cut, then right-click the
        destination header and choose <strong>Insert Cut Cells</strong>.
      </p>
      <p>
        To drag <strong>rows</strong> instead, first copy Grid!A1:E4 and use{" "}
        <strong>Paste Special → Values + Transpose</strong> on another blank sheet. Original columns
        become rows, with labels 1–5 in the first column. Reorder those complete rows with
        Shift-drag or Insert Cut Cells. Transpose back when finished so you can read the message
        across its blocks.
      </p>
      <p>
        Use <strong>View → Freeze Panes → Freeze Top Row</strong> to keep column labels visible, or
        Freeze First Column in the transposed layout. This affects scrolling only. Browser and Mac
        Excel may have different drag gestures; Insert Cut Cells avoids relying on them.
      </p>
      <p>
        For block transposition, read each row left to right, then move to the next row. To join a
        row on the Manual sheet, put <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A2:E2)</code> in G2, fill down, then
        join G2:G4 with <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,G2:G4)</code>. Adjust the last row for longer
        messages.
      </p>
      <h2 id="key">Automate the order with a key</h2>
      <p>
        For an automatic route, keep the original Grid unchanged. In Output!A1:E1 enter{" "}
        <strong>5, 3, 1, 2, 4</strong> as separate numbers. In Output!A2 enter:
      </p>
      <pre>=INDEX(Grid!$A2:$E2,1,A$1)</pre>
      <p>
        Fill across five columns and down to row 4. Each output column now selects an original
        column by its number. In G2 use <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A2:E2)</code>, fill down to G4, then
        join G2:G4 for block output. Join each whole column instead to get columnar output: in A6
        use <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A2:A4)</code>, fill across to E6, then{" "}
        <code>=TEXTJOIN("",TRUE,A6:E6)</code>.
      </p>
      <h3>Numeric key from one cell</h3>
      <p>
        If Setup!B4 contains the text <code>53124</code>, Output!A1 can use the following, filled
        across. It supports single-digit columns only; for 10 or more, enter the column numbers
        separately.
      </p>
      <pre>=VALUE(MID(Setup!$B$4,COLUMN(),1))</pre>
      <h3>Word key: HOTEL → 41523</h3>
      <p>
        Write HOTEL across the original columns. Alphabetical order is E, H, L, O, T, originally at
        positions 4, 1, 5, 2, 3. Thus the read order is <code>41523</code>. This is different from
        writing each letter's alphabetical rank under the original word, which gives 2, 4, 5, 1, 3.
      </p>
      <pre>Original column: 1 2 3 4 5
Word key:        H O T E L
Read order:      4 1 5 2 3</pre>
      <p>
        In Excel 365 / 2024, put HOTEL in Setup!B4 and this formula in Output!A1. Leave A1:E1
        otherwise empty so the result can spill:
      </p>
      <pre>=LET(k,UPPER(Setup!$B$4),p,SEQUENCE(1,LEN(k)),SORTBY(p,MID(k,p,1),1,p,1))</pre>
      <p>
        For duplicate letters, the original position breaks ties left to right. For earlier Excel
        versions, sort the key letters manually, keeping original positions next to them. Validate
        any numeric key: every original column number must appear exactly once.
      </p>
      <h2 id="decipher">Reverse the process</h2>
      <p>
        For <strong>block transposition</strong>, write ciphertext across rows and use the inverse
        key. For encryption order 5, 3, 1, 2, 4 the inverse is <strong>3, 4, 2, 5, 1</strong>: the
        original column 1 is now in position 3, column 2 in position 4, and so on. In an empty area
        you can calculate this with <code>=MATCH(COLUMN(),$A$1:$E$1,0)</code>, filled across.
      </p>
      <p>
        <strong>An incomplete final block needs its own inverse.</strong> With 14 letters, the last
        block has four letters. Drop missing original column 5 from 53124, giving 3124 for that row;
        its inverse is 2314. Do not apply the full five-column inverse to a short row. The site's
        tool handles this automatically.
      </p>
      <p>
        For <strong>columnar transposition</strong>, first split ciphertext into original columns in
        key order. If there are L letters and N columns, every column has <code>INT(L/N)</code>{" "}
        letters; the first <code>MOD(L,N)</code> original columns have one extra. Fill each column
        top to bottom, then read rows across in original order. For 14 letters and five columns,
        original columns 1–4 have three letters, column 5 has two. With 53124, take chunks of
        lengths 2, 3, 3, 3, 3. The workbench can verify your reconstruction.
      </p>
      <h2 id="check">Check with a known message</h2>
      <ol>
        <li>Start with WEAREDISCOVERED, block size 5, key 53124.</li>
        <li>
          Block generation should produce <code>EAWEROSDICDRVEE</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
          Columnar generation should produce <code>EODASRWDVEIERCE</code>.
        </li>
        <li>Reverse either method and check that every letter returns.</li>
        <li>Try HOTEL, then BALLOON to check a word key with duplicates.</li>
      </ol>
      <p class="small">
        Excel references:{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-gb/excel/move-or-copy-cells-rows-and-columns?nochrome=true">
          Microsoft: move or copy rows and columns
        </a>
        ,{" "}
        <a href="https://support.microsoft.com/en-us/office/freeze-panes-to-lock-rows-and-columns-dab2ffc9-020d-4026-8121-67dd25f2508f">
          Freeze Panes
        </a>
        . The formulas and examples above are built for this session.
      </p>
      <div class="actions">
        <a class="button primary" href={`${base}/tools/#transposition`}>
          Try transposition ↗
        </a>
        <a class="text-link" href={`${base}/decks/transposition/`}>
          Open this deck →
        </a>
      </div>
    </article>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
